WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:10.338
Hi! Adjectives and adverbs make sentences lively. Today we'll learn how to compare, and how to use too and enough.

2
00:00:10.488 --> 00:00:15.978
Adjectives describe nouns. In a quick runner, quick describes the runner.

3
00:00:16.128 --> 00:00:23.970
Adverbs describe verbs, telling us how an action is done. In She runs quickly, quickly describes runs.

4
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:30.954
Many adverbs are adjectives plus -ly: quick becomes quickly, careful becomes carefully.

5
00:00:31.104 --> 00:00:37.578
But the adverb of good is well. She sings well, not She sings good.

6
00:00:37.728 --> 00:00:48.786
To compare two things, use the comparative. For the most out of three or more, use the superlative. Short words add -er and -est: tall, taller, tallest.

7
00:00:48.936 --> 00:00:55.842
Short words ending consonant, vowel, consonant double the last letter: big, bigger, biggest.

8
00:00:55.992 --> 00:01:01.146
Words ending in y change y to i: happy, happier, happiest.

9
00:01:01.296 --> 00:01:13.410
Longer words use more and most: more careful, most careful. And there are irregular ones: good, better, best, and bad, worse, worst.

10
00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:24.162
Too goes before an adjective. It means more than is good, often with a bad result. The box is too heavy to lift: it's so heavy that we can't lift it.

11
00:01:24.312 --> 00:01:32.202
Enough goes after an adjective. It means sufficient. She is tall enough to reach the shelf: she can reach it.

12
00:01:32.352 --> 00:01:39.930
In the negative: He is not old enough to drive. He isn't old enough yet, so he can't drive.

13
00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.538
Watch out! Ali is more taller than Ben is wrong!

14
00:01:44.688 --> 00:01:51.474
Taller is already the comparative, so we can't add more. Say: Ali is taller than Ben.

15
00:01:51.624 --> 00:01:59.082
Remember: short words add -er, long words use more, but never both together.

16
00:01:59.232 --> 00:02:09.354
Your turn! This is the ___ day of the year. Hot, hotter or hottest? Pause and think.

17
00:02:09.504 --> 00:02:19.050
The answer is hottest. It's the hottest of the whole year, so we use the superlative, with a double t. Did you get it right?

18
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:32.394
Another one! The water is ___ cold ___ swim in. It means the water is so cold that we can't swim. What goes in? Pause and think.

19
00:02:32.544 --> 00:02:38.178
The answer is too cold to swim in. Did you get it right?

20
00:02:38.328 --> 00:02:47.826
Last one! My sister draws very ___. Good or well? Pause and think.

21
00:02:47.976 --> 00:02:55.842
The answer is well, because it describes the verb draws, so we need an adverb. Did you get it right?

22
00:02:55.992 --> 00:03:11.298
Remember: adjectives describe nouns and adverbs describe verbs; the adverb of good is well. Use a comparative with than for two things, and the plus a superlative for the most. Too goes before an adjective, and enough goes after it.

23
00:03:11.448 --> 00:03:15.810
Find out who is the tallest in your family!

